{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789234903,"nonce":"63PvZvuCxZiNTEKbx9OnKoyWyNU=","title":"Errors raised inside compiled code or a nested run loop escape Fiber.try","message":"Three gaps in how a runtime error travels from where it is raised to the enclosing Fiber.try:\n\n1. The interpreter dispatches into compiled code (closure dispatch, the threaded tier, the root-frame native path, an OSR entry) and never checks vm.has_error afterwards. The flag lingers until an unrelated later native call trips over it, on whatever fiber is current by then, so the error surfaces as a hard error at the wrong place with no location.\n\n2. A nested, depth-bounded run loop (compiled code calling an interpreted callee through call_closure_sync) routed the error through Fiber.try itself, switching fibers underneath the compiled caller that was waiting for one frame to unwind. The caller then continued as if the callee had returned. Reproduced intermittently in the e2e suite at 8 threads once the MIR inliner made compiled-to-interpreted calls common.\n\n3. call_closure_sync and the constructor bridges dropped the Err from the nested loop (result.ok()), so once the loop unwound instead of routing, the abort message was lost and a later unrelated raise replaced it (test_fiber_abort_through_closure_preserves_subsequent_calls saw 'Right operand must be a number.' instead of 'bad').\n\nFix: every interpreter-to-compiled boundary consumes the flag; a nested loop unwinds errors on the fiber it was entered on (vm.sync_entry_fiber) and routing happens only at the level that owns the fiber; the bridges re-raise.","files":null}]}